# Test: table IDs and route metrics at the top of the uint32 range stay valid.
#
# VALIDATES: the full kernel-legal uint32 range remains accepted for a policy
#            route `then table`, a named routing-table id, and a static route
#            `metric`, on the 64-bit targets Ze builds.
# PREVENTS: the netlink-encodability bound added for CodeQL alert 170
#           (go/incorrect-integer-conversion) being tightened to a hardcoded
#           32-bit constant. That bound exists because netlink.Rule.Table,
#           netlink.Route.Table and netlink.Route.Priority are Go ints whose
#           attributes are dropped when negative, so it must track this build's
#           own math.MaxInt and must not narrow the range on a 64-bit build.
#
# NOTE: the rejection side of that bound cannot be exercised here. On a 64-bit
# build math.MaxInt is above every uint32, so no config value can trip it. It is
# covered by unit tests that pass the bound explicitly:
# TestRejectUnencodableTableID, TestValidateActionTableRejectsUnencodable,
# TestValidateRouteMetricRejectsUnencodable.

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
routing-table {
    table upstream {
        id 4294967295;
    }
}
policy {
    route wan-steer {
        interface eth0;
        rule bulk {
            from {
                protocol tcp;
            }
            then {
                table 4294967294;
            }
        }
    }
}
static {
    table default {
        route 192.0.2.0/24 {
            metric 4294967295;
            next {
                hop 10.0.0.1 { }
            }
        }
    }
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze config validate -:stdin=config
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=configuration valid
